# THE CAPACITY BUFFERS FILED AS EMPTY. One row per (filing, layer) whose capacity slack is a
# stated zero.
#
# ⭐⭐ A SUPPLY THAT CANNOT RUN HOT. If the capacity buffer is empty and the filer SAID SO,
#    then demand above the nameplate CANNOT have been absorbed by running the supply harder.
#    It went unserved, and somebody must be named as having gone without. That is the whole
#    argument the two rules downstream rest on.
#
# ✅⛔⛔ AND IT RESTS ON `stated`, NOT ON `measured`, WHICH IS WHAT THIS FILE USED TO CLAIM.
#    Once every stated zero carried a `boundOrigin` the population could be read back, and
#    NOT ONE of the eleven was measured: eight `contractual` -- a reserved block somebody
#    negotiated -- and three `intrinsic` -- a material limit. Nobody checked a buffer and
#    found it empty.
#
# ⭐ THE THREE ORIGINS ARE ALL SUFFICIENT AND THAT IS THE POINT. A shelf life entails a zero,
#    a reserved block negotiates one, and an observation records one. All three are somebody
#    saying the buffer is empty and standing behind it. An ABSENT slack licenses nothing --
#    and that is equally true of a buffer nobody measured and of one nobody CAN.
#
# ✅⛔ THE DISJUNCTION IS GONE, NOT REFACTORED. This composed a `:union(ALL ...)` of two
#    spellings while a zero could be filed as `absent reason="none"`. `pm:ClaimAbsence` has
#    no `none`, so there is one spelling and a union over one arm is a lie about the model.
-- pm:Nameplate/pm:capacitySlack, zero either way.
SELECT z.filing, z.layer, z.high AS zero
FROM (
    :compose(entries/zero_by_claim.sqlc)
) z
WHERE z.buffer = 'capacity'
